The relative effectiveness of longbows vs. crossbows is still open to
debate.  A recent study using wrought iron plate based on historical
suits in the Tower of London (I assume the lighter field suits, not
the heavy tournament ones) and _wrought_iron_ arrowheads found that
plate was quite resistent to longbows at relatively close range, i.e.
the range at which the longbowmen would have run.  Damage to the limbs
could be extensive, but was quite limited to the trunk and head.  Chain
wouldn't fair very well, though.  Crossbows could be made more powerful
and were easier to use, but were expensive, so crossbowmen tended to be
professionals as well.  The bolts also had better carrying power than
arrows did (Sci. Am. had a nice article some years back on this)
Crossbows and recurved compound bows would have a tendency to delaminate
in a dungeon, though, making them dangerous to use, while a dried out
longbow would lose much of its force, which might happen from fire...

